# Feature preflight — MVP, art, design

Before planning or implementing a **new feature, screen, mechanic, or product task**, read the relevant parts of these sources. Do not invent scope or look.

1. **MVP scope** — `docs/mvp.md` (Section 10 = out of scope). Then `docs/sprint-current.md` for what this sprint actually builds.
2. **Art style** — `docs/art-style-guide.md` (characters, worlds, palette, motion, UI vs illustration). Moodboard: `docs/moodboard/README.md`.
3. **Design / UI** — `.cursor/skills/sweet-spot-screen-style/SKILL.md`, tokens in `my-expo-app/theme/artStyle.ts` and `my-expo-app/theme/brand.ts`. For screens or UX, also follow `.cursor/skills/ui-ux/SKILL.md`. Level mechanics: `docs/Question_Templates.md`.

## How to use them

- Open the files with `@` / Read. Do not dump the whole spec into chat.
- If the request is outside MVP Section 10, refuse to build it and say so.
- If a mockup, plugin, or skill (including generic design skills) conflicts with the art style or tokens, the repo guides win.
- Spec conflicts still follow `docs-hierarchy`: MVP > current sprint > feature docs.

## Phone app, not a website

Every feature is designed for a **phone app** (Expo / React Native), not for the web.

- Primary target: a phone in the hand. Touch, thumb reach, safe areas, portrait, hit targets ≥44pt.
- Do not ship desktop/web layouts, hover-only controls, `cursor-pointer`, CSS frameworks, or a separate website for the same feature.
- Expo web (if used) is only a preview of the **same phone UI**. It must still look and behave like the mobile app, not a redesigned web product.

## Skip the full visual read when

The task is purely mechanical and cannot change product look or scope (typo, git, CI, comments). Still do not add out-of-scope features.
